<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Bukhara</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 750 CE (approx.) · capital Bukhara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sogdian city-state ruled by the Bukhar Khudahs, whose queen-regent Khatun faced the Arab conquest of Transoxiana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in the Zarafshan delta west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, was ruled from the 6th or 7th century by a dynasty bearing the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudah</a>, lord of Bukhara. Tradition, recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narshakhi">Narshakhi</a>, links its foundation to settlers and princes from the Hephthalite and Turkic period.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudahs</a> ruled from the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> over an oasis of farms and walled estates. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, imitating the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-v">Bahram V</a>, circulated widely as 'Bukhar Khudah drachms' for centuries. The regent known as Khatun ruled in the late 7th century during the Arab raids, alternately paying tribute and fighting.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 709 and installed the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughshada">Tughshada</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudah</a>, building the first mosque. The dynasty kept a ceremonial role until its last members were killed around 782, and Bukhara later became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 600 CE – 750 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.64%;width:2.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 750 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bukhara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khatun, Tughshada</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sogdian Zoroastrianism, later Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sogdian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Uzbekistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bukhar Khudahs, Bukhara (Sogdian)</dd></dl></aside></div>

